## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about small logical constraints and translate them into a checkable search, including vacuous implications and cardinality.

## Key Concepts

- Logical implications and vacuous truth
- Cardinality constraints (minimum true)
- Backtracking with pruning
- Generalizing to N variables

## Code Example

```javascript
function countTrue(arr){ return arr.filter(x=>x).length; }
function implies(p,q){ return !p || q; }
function sat(assignment){
  const [A,B,C] = assignment;
  if(!implies(A,B)) return false;
  if(!implies(B,C)) return false;
  if(countTrue(assignment) < 2) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to 4+ vars with arbitrary implications?
- How would you optimize for sparse constraint graphs and avoid enumerating all 2^n assignments?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design and analyze dynamic constraint propagation with 3-valued domains, going beyond static logic. The problem requires constructing a concrete propagation trace and a fixed point that preserves an unknown value, mirroring real-world policy engines.

## Key Concepts
- Forward-propagation with 3-valued domains
- Fixed-point convergence
- Handling U without monotone destruction
- Traceable propagation: demonstrate steps and termination

## Code Example
```javascript
function applyRules(state){
  // state: {X1,X2,X3,X4} values in {T,F,U}
  // R1
  if(state.X1==="T" && state.X2==="T") state.X3="T";
  // R2
  if(state.X4==="F") state.X1="F";
  // R3
  if(state.X3==="F") {
    if(state.X2!=="F" && state.X4!=="F") { /* no change */ }
  }
  // R4
  if(state.X1==="U" || state.X2==="U") state.X4="U";
  return state;
}
```

